LEGACY -> CURRENT: the same exposure, expressed as Ingress and as Gateway API.

Left:  examples/ingress-legacy/ingress.yaml      (networking.k8s.io/v1 Ingress)
Right: examples/k8s/05-tls/30-gateway.yaml + 60-routes.yaml (gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1)

The shapes do not line up one-to-one, and that is the point: one Ingress object
mixes listener configuration, TLS and routing, which Gateway API splits across
Gateway (cluster operator) and HTTPRoute (application team). Read the diff as
"what happens to each field", not as a mechanical rename.

--- a/examples/ingress-legacy/ingress.yaml
+++ b/examples/k8s/05-tls/30-gateway.yaml
@@
-apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
-kind: IngressClass
-metadata:
-  name: traefik
-spec:
-  controller: traefik.io/ingress-controller
+# IngressClass -> GatewayClass. Same job: name the controller that owns this
+# traffic. GatewayClass adds parametersRef, a typed pointer to
+# implementation-specific settings, which on Ingress had to be annotations.
+apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
+kind: GatewayClass
+metadata:
+  name: eg
+spec:
+  controllerName: gateway.envoyproxy.io/gatewayclass-controller
+  parametersRef:
+    group: gateway.envoyproxy.io
+    kind: EnvoyProxy
+    name: kind-nodeport
+    namespace: envoy-gateway-system
@@
-apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
-kind: Ingress
-metadata:
-  name: tasklane-api
-  namespace: tasklane
-  annotations:
-    cert-manager.io/cluster-issuer: tasklane-ca
-spec:
-  ingressClassName: traefik
-  tls:
-    - hosts:
-        - tasklane.localhost
-      secretName: tasklane-tls-ingress
+# spec.tls -> a Gateway listener. Ports are explicit now: an Ingress never
+# said which port it listened on, the controller decided. TLS mode is explicit
+# too (Terminate vs Passthrough), and the certificate is a typed reference
+# rather than a bare secret name.
+apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
+kind: Gateway
+metadata:
+  name: tasklane
+  namespace: tasklane
+spec:
+  gatewayClassName: eg
+  listeners:
+    - name: http
+      protocol: HTTP
+      port: 80
+      allowedRoutes:
+        namespaces:
+          from: Same
+    - name: https
+      protocol: HTTPS
+      port: 443
+      hostname: tasklane.localhost
+      tls:
+        mode: Terminate
+        certificateRefs:
+          - kind: Secret
+            group: ""
+            name: tasklane-tls
+      allowedRoutes:
+        namespaces:
+          from: Same

--- a/examples/ingress-legacy/ingress.yaml
+++ b/examples/k8s/05-tls/60-routes.yaml
@@
-  rules:
-    - host: tasklane.localhost
-      http:
-        paths:
-          - path: /
-            pathType: Prefix
-            backend:
-              service:
-                name: tasklane-api
-                port:
-                  number: 80
+# spec.rules -> HTTPRoute. hostnames is a list, parentRefs attaches the route
+# to one or more Gateways (and optionally a named listener), pathType: Prefix
+# becomes path.type: PathPrefix, and the backend becomes backendRefs - plural,
+# with weights.
+apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
+kind: HTTPRoute
+metadata:
+  name: tasklane-api
+  namespace: tasklane
+spec:
+  parentRefs:
+    - name: tasklane
+      sectionName: http
+    - name: tasklane
+      sectionName: https
+  rules:
+    - matches:
+        - path:
+            type: PathPrefix
+            value: /
+      backendRefs:
+        - name: tasklane-api
+          port: 80
+          weight: 9
+        - name: tasklane-api-canary
+          port: 80
+          weight: 1

What has no Ingress equivalent at all
=====================================

  Ingress                              Gateway API
  -----------------------------------  ----------------------------------------
  redirect-to-HTTPS annotation,        RequestRedirect filter, in the API, same
  different in every controller        behaviour on every implementation
  canary/weight annotations            backendRefs[].weight
  header/method routing annotations    matches[].headers, matches[].method
  rewrite annotations                  URLRewrite filter
  (nothing)                            GRPCRoute, TLSRoute, TCPRoute, UDPRoute
  (nothing)                            ReferenceGrant: cross-namespace consent
  (nothing)                            BackendTLSPolicy: TLS to the backend
  (nothing)                            typed status conditions per listener and
                                       per parent (Accepted, Programmed,
                                       ResolvedRefs)
  cross-namespace backends impossible  allowedRoutes + ReferenceGrant

Tooling: ingress2gateway (1.0, 2026-03-20) converts existing Ingress objects,
including several controllers' annotations, into Gateway and HTTPRoute objects.
Treat its output as a first draft - review every listener and hostname.
